<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suppressions PUBLIC
    "-//Checkstyle//DTD SuppressionFilter Configuration 1.2//EN"
    "https://checkstyle.org/dtds/suppressions_1_2.dtd">

<!--
=============================================================================
config/checkstyle/suppressions.xml
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Purpose:
  The companion file to config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, and the ONLY place in
  this repository where an exemption from the Java documentation gate may be
  recorded. That gate mechanically enforces this project's single
  user-specified rule, Rule 1 (Explainability), for Java across every Maven
  module under services/.

  How this file is reached: checkstyle.xml declares a SuppressionFilter whose
  file is ${config_loc}/suppressions.xml with its optional flag false, and
  services/pom.xml supplies config_loc as the directory holding the ruleset and
  binds maven-checkstyle-plugin to the validate phase with failOnViolation
  true. The POM deliberately does NOT set the plugin's own
  suppressions-location parameter, so this file is loaded through the ruleset's
  filter and through nothing else. Addressing it relative to the ruleset is
  what makes it resolve identically from the services/ aggregator and from each
  module directory one level deeper.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Scope and how to add an entry:
  The sanctioned surface is generated sources and test fixture material, and
  nothing else. An entry must put its justification in an XML comment
  IMMEDIATELY ABOVE the element, tag it with at least one of Rule 1's four
  categories in the one accepted spelling ("Alternatives Considered:",
  "Refactoring Rationale:", "Assumptions:" or "Trade-offs:", as
  docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md sets out), narrow itself with a checks
  attribute naming only checks the ruleset configures, and name the broader
  alternative it rejected together with the concrete consequence of having
  taken it.

  Checks available to name, and only these ten: JavadocPackage,
  MissingJavadocType, MissingJavadocMethod, MissingJavadocPackage,
  JavadocType, JavadocMethod, NonEmptyAtclauseDescription, SummaryJavadoc,
  AtclauseOrder, CommentsIndentation.

  Assumptions: those ten are the WHOLE set checkstyle.xml enables, and the list is
    written out rather than abbreviated so the two files can be compared name for
    name. That ruleset configures thirteen module elements, of which Checker,
    TreeWalker and SuppressionFilter are a container, a container and a filter,
    leaving exactly the ten documentation checks named above.
  Assumptions: the practical consequence on the two sanctioned paths is worth
    stating rather than leaving to be discovered. Every check the ruleset
    configures is a documentation or comment check, so a `.java` file under
    target/generated-sources/ or beside a fixture has its documentation
    obligation waived and carries no other obligation this gate could relax. The
    documentation obligation is waived where there is no author to ask, and
    nothing else is waived because nothing else is enforced here.
  Assumptions: naming a check outside the ten is worse than useless. A
    suppression for an unconfigured check never fires, so nothing reveals that
    it is dead, while the entry still reads to the next maintainer as evidence
    that the check is active and had to be relaxed. SingleLineJavadoc,
    JavadocVariable, JavadocStyle and WriteTag are the likeliest mistakes:
    each is deliberately absent from the ruleset, with its reason recorded in
    that file's EXCLUDED MODULES section. RedundantImport and UnusedImports are
    the same mistake for a different reason: both were configured for a time and
    are now withdrawn, so naming either one would read as evidence of an import
    gate this project does not operate.
  Refactoring Rationale: this section briefly described the ten-name list as a
    SUBSET, on the reading that RedundantImport and UnusedImports were also
    configured. Both have since been withdrawn from the ruleset, which is
    chartered documentation-only, so the ten names are once again the complete
    set and the list is described that way. The count is stated together with
    the element arithmetic above, because a maintainer who counts declarations
    rather than reading names reaches thirteen and would otherwise take the
    difference for a defect in this file.
  Assumptions: the justification sits in an adjacent comment because the DTD
    leaves no alternative. The suppress element is declared EMPTY and its
    attribute list admits only files, checks, message, id, lines and columns; a
    reason attribute is not among them, so the widely copied idiom that writes
    one onto the element makes the document invalid against the DTD and, because
    the filter fails closed, stops the reactor before a single class is
    compiled. Worse than the breakage is the near miss: an entry that looks
    documented while the attribute carrying its reason is silently unusable.
  Assumptions: every entry narrows by checks and none narrows by message.
    Checkstyle's own documentation warns that filtering by message is dependent
    on runtime locale, so a build under a different locale silently stops
    matching, and an entry then either stops suppressing what it was written
    for or starts suppressing something adjacent. Check names carry no such
    dependency.
  Trade-offs: each checks value spells out all ten documentation names rather
    than abbreviating them to a pattern such as Javadoc.* plus the leftovers. A
    wildcard would silently absorb any check added to the ruleset later, so the
    exemption would widen with no edit to this file and no review, and matching
    is performed against the fully qualified class name so a wildcard's true
    reach is not readable from the pattern. The cost is a long attribute value.
    That cost has already been repaid once: because both entries enumerate,
    RedundantImport and UnusedImports arriving in the ruleset and later leaving
    it widened this file's reach by nothing at all in either direction, whereas a
    Javadoc.*-plus-leftovers pattern written as a catch-all would have had to be
    re-audited on both occasions to establish the same thing.
  Trade-offs: exactly two entries are declared and neither is speculative.
    Pre-loading every path the architecture can be predicted to want was
    rejected, because a suppression for a tree that holds no files cannot be
    reviewed: nobody can tell whether it is still needed, whether its pattern
    is as tight as it should be, or whether it has quietly begun matching
    hand-authored code.
  Assumptions: this file failing closed is deliberate and is relied upon from
    two sides; checkstyle.xml sets optional to false and services/pom.xml
    records that the flag must not be worked around. With optional true and the
    file absent, Checkstyle's documented behaviour is that the filter accepts
    all audit events, so the build would go green having suppressed nothing and
    having verified nothing. The practical consequence is narrow and important:
    emptying this file back to a bare suppressions element is supported;
    deleting the file is not.
  Assumptions: the house comment idiom cannot be reproduced literally here,
    because XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere inside
    a comment. The house files use a hyphen mid-rule and a spaced double hyphen
    as an em-dash; neither survives, so the mid-rules are tildes and the
    em-dash is a single hyphen, a colon or a semicolon. Restoring either makes
    the file unparseable, and the filter fails closed.
=============================================================================
  -->

<suppressions>

  <!--
    Generated sources: code emitted by a build plugin, never authored.
      Assumptions: the exemption is legitimate because Rule 1 governs AUTHORED
        code. The rule asks an author to state a purpose, the parameters, the
        return value and the exceptions; generated code has no author to ask, so
        this is the boundary of the rule's applicability rather than a
        relaxation of it, and it mirrors the boundary already drawn around the
        reference-only COBOL baseline. The pattern is scoped to the
        target/generated-sources/ directory segment pair rather than to target/
        as a whole, so it cannot reach a hand-authored tree even if build output
        is ever redirected, and it names all ten DOCUMENTATION checks because a
        generated file would trip the presence and completeness checks alike. It
        names NEITHER import check, so a generated file carrying a duplicate or
        unreferenced import still fails; that half of the ruleset is not
        relaxed here, for the reason recorded in the Scope section above.
      Trade-offs: this entry is a GUARD and is not currently load-bearing, which
        is stated rather than glossed. The gate is bound to the validate phase,
        which runs before generate-sources, so no generator has added output to
        the compile source roots when the audit executes. It becomes
        load-bearing if that binding moves to a later phase, or if the
        per-service OpenAPI 3.1 contracts are wired to generate stubs or DTOs
        whose root is registered by then; it is kept for that case rather than
        removed, because the alternative is discovering the need as a build
        failure with no record of why the exemption is correct.
      Alternatives Considered: writing the same exclusion as an excludes entry
        on maven-checkstyle-plugin in services/pom.xml. Rejected because it
        splits exemption policy across two files: this file would no longer be
        the complete record of what is exempt, and a reviewer reading it would
        draw a false conclusion from its shortness.
  -->
  <suppress files="[\\/]target[\\/]generated-sources[\\/]"
            checks="JavadocPackage|MissingJavadocType|MissingJavadocMethod|MissingJavadocPackage|JavadocType|JavadocMethod|NonEmptyAtclauseDescription|SummaryJavadoc|AtclauseOrder|CommentsIndentation"/>

  <!--
    Test fixture material: records derived from the copybook layouts.
      Assumptions: fixtures under src/test/resources/fixtures/ are DATA, not
        behaviour. A fixture record is a fixed-width image of an app/cpy/
        copybook layout, and its meaning lives in that layout, at the declared
        record length, rather than in a docstring attached to it; asking for a
        purpose, parameters and a return value from a 300-byte account image has
        no answer to give. It names all ten DOCUMENTATION checks for the same
        reason as the entry above, and neither import check for the same reason
        either.
      Trade-offs: this entry is DEFENSIVE and may never fire. checkstyle.xml
        sets fileExtensions to java, so the fixed-width fixture files under
        resources/ are already outside the audit set; what remains is the narrow
        case of a .java file generated into, or co-located with, that fixtures
        directory.
      Alternatives Considered: the broader form, a single entry on src/test/.
        Rejected outright, and this is the boundary that matters most about this
        entry: it covers src/test/resources/fixtures/ and must never be widened
        to src/test/java/. Widening it would exempt every controller, service
        and repository test in every module, and those tests are where the
        business rules transcribed from the COBOL are actually asserted.
        services/pom.xml sets includeTestSourceDirectory to true so that they
        are audited; an entry on src/test/ would undo that setting from this
        side while leaving it apparently in force.
  -->
  <suppress files="[\\/]src[\\/]test[\\/]resources[\\/]fixtures[\\/]"
            checks="JavadocPackage|MissingJavadocType|MissingJavadocMethod|MissingJavadocPackage|JavadocType|JavadocMethod|NonEmptyAtclauseDescription|SummaryJavadoc|AtclauseOrder|CommentsIndentation"/>

  <!--
=============================================================================
NEVER SUPPRESS: what must not be added to this file, and the consequence.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Alternatives Considered: every item below is a plausible entry that was
  evaluated and rejected. They are written out because the two entries above
  are the readable part of this file and the dangerous part is invisible: an
  exemption nobody wrote. Nothing here is a suppress element, and nothing here
  should become one.

1. services/*/src/main/java/ : NEVER, not one entry and not one pattern that
   could match it. This is the hand-authored production code the gate exists to
   police, and exempting any part of it leaves the build reporting success over
   code Rule 1 no longer reaches, indistinguishable from a genuine pass.

2. A mapper package : NEVER, and this is the trap most likely to be walked
   into, because "mapper" reads as "generated" in most codebases. Here it is
   the opposite: the mappers are the hand-written anti-corruption layer, and
   MapStruct was rejected on the recorded ground that copybook-to-DTO mapping
   is not mechanical; it drops FILLER, masks the primary account number to its
   last four digits, suppresses the card verification value, encrypts the
   national and government-issued identifiers, and renames three misspelled
   baseline fields. Each of those is a decision a reader cannot recover from the
   code, which makes a mapper the highest-value documentation target in the
   repository. checkstyle.xml widens type scope to private for this exact
   population, and because Checkstyle's scope property is inclusive downwards, a
   private nested mapper helper is audited on the same terms as the mapper.

3. A service Application class or a config package : NEVER. These are the
   module entry point and configuration types Rule 1's presence clause names
   outright. The sibling route to the same exemption is closed on purpose:
   checkstyle.xml declines to set skipAnnotations even though the upstream
   example for MissingJavadocType sets it to SpringBootApplication and
   Configuration, which would exempt every entry point and config package.

4. The test tree as a whole : NEVER. Tests are in scope for Rule 1, the house
   convention requires a docstring on every test, fixture builder, helper, mock
   and runner routine, and services/pom.xml turns on test-source auditing.
   Only genuinely mechanical fixture material may be narrowed, which is what
   the second entry does and the precise reason it stops at resources/.

5. A catch-all : NEVER. A files value of .*, or an element carrying neither a
   files nor a checks attribute, exempts everything from everything. The DTD
   permits it because every attribute is optional, so nothing but review stands
   in the way, and one such element silently disables the whole gate while
   leaving the ruleset apparently in force.

6. An in-code bypass : NEVER, and it cannot be added here at all, which is the
   point. The ruleset configures no SuppressWarningsFilter, no
   SuppressionCommentFilter and no SuppressWithNearbyCommentFilter, so a
   suppression cannot be written into a Java source as an annotation or a magic
   comment. That omission is what gives this file its value: every exemption is
   a durable entry here and a reviewer can read the complete list in one place.

Assumptions: append a new entry directly beneath the second one and above this
  block. Do not delete the enclosing suppressions element when the last entry
  goes: the file must stay present and parseable, because the filter that reads
  it fails closed.
=============================================================================
  -->

</suppressions>
